        era: "2000 – today",
        subtitle: "A country of many nationalities, three languages, and outsize influence",
        hook: `<h4 class="hook-h">A country half foreign</h4>
<p>Today <strong>46.6%</strong> of Luxembourg's ~691,000 residents are foreign nationals — from about <strong>180 countries</strong> (2026). Every working day, another <strong>~237,000 cross-border workers</strong> arrive from France (~half), Belgium and Germany. Close to half of the workforce lives outside Luxembourg. Without them, the economy stops.</p>
<h4 class="hook-h">Three languages, one country</h4>
<p>Three languages are official (since the 1984 law): <strong>Luxembourgish</strong> is the national language, <strong>French</strong> is the language of legislation, and <strong>German</strong> is the administrative and school-literacy language. It's the reason your naturalisation only requires <strong>A2 speaking and B1 listening</strong> in Luxembourgish — not full fluency.</p>
<h4 class="hook-h">The 2023 constitutional revision</h4>
<p>On <strong>1 July 2023</strong> the revised Constitution entered into force — the first complete rewrite of the 1868 text. Fundamental rights were rewritten, climate and animal welfare joined the constitutional objectives, the Grand Duke lost the free right to dissolve the Chamber, and a National Council of Justice was created. Since <strong>2009</strong>, <strong>dual citizenship</strong> has been permitted, and since the <strong>2017</strong> nationality law you can apply after <strong>5 years</strong> of residence — you can keep your current passport when you naturalise (subject to your other country's law). And in <strong>2020</strong>, Luxembourg became the first country in the world with <strong>free public transport</strong> nationwide.</p>
<h4 class="hook-h">A throne changes hands</h4>
<p><strong>Grand Duke Henri</strong> reigned from <strong>7 October 2000</strong>. In 2024 he named his son <strong>Guillaume</strong> lieutenant-représentant as a transition step, and on <strong>3 October 2025</strong> he abdicated. The same day <strong>Grand Duke Guillaume</strong> took the constitutional oath before the Chamber — the current head of state. Expect the exam to ask who the Grand Duke is; expect older study material to still say Henri.</p>`,
